# Backend Spec

## Scope

Go API, domain boundaries, routes, persistence, cache, security, store/auth, and
the embedded immutable KJV corpus contracts.

## Load when

Changing backend handlers/services/repositories, routes, models, migrations,
auth, store, cache, WebSocket emission, uploads, SSR, or grengo proxy behavior.

## Owns

- Entry/router: `backend/main.go`
- Models: `backend/models/*.go`
- Domains: `backend/internal/{auth,user,externalidentity,forum,documentation,store,inbox,notification,config,page,datasource,customsection,events,upload,ws,ssr,grengo,bible}`
- DB/Redis: `backend/database/db.go`
- Migrations: `backend/internal/migrations/*.sql`

## Architecture Contracts

- Go 1.24, chi/v5, raw SQL via `lib/pq`; no ORM.
- Domain shape is `handler => service => repository`.
- Handlers decode/validate HTTP and auth context only.
- Services own business rules, cache invalidation, event dispatch, and WS pushes.
- Repositories execute SQL, accept `context.Context` where the local interface
  does, and avoid business logic.
- Interfaces live per domain in `interface.go` for test substitution.
- `internal/bible` embeds the pinned KJV JSON corpus, translation metadata, and
  66 rendering-marker sidecars. Before the router starts it validates 66 books,
  1,189 chapters, 31,102 verses, consecutive coordinates, non-empty text,
  metadata identity, exact marker alignment/span bounds, the aggregate corpus
  SHA-512, and Skaia's metadata/marker snapshot SHA-512.
- Redis caches are best-effort, tenant-prefixed, read-through, and invalidated on writes.
- SSR SEO uses a typed success/absence/degraded/error resolution boundary. Only
  authoritative success and genuine absence are cacheable; core SQL/config
  errors return uncached `503`, while optional enrichment errors serve safe base
  metadata uncached. `sql.ErrNoRows` is the SQL absence boundary.
- The SSR handler reads `INDEX_FILE_PATH` on every response so startup failure is
  retryable and `grengo ship frontend` replacements are observed without a
  backend restart. Template failures return `503`, `Retry-After`, and noindex.
- `pages.content` is the sole custom-page definition and interactive-record
  authority. Saves accept the observed numeric/string IDs and string/object
  config encodings, preserve unknown fields, and validate registered types,
  integrations, components, and interactive definitions.
- Its allowlist contains 20 renderer types. The generic resource embed keeps
  only a supported resource type plus a product/thread identifier or
  documentation slug in `pages.content` and resolves data through the
  existing source-domain APIs and policies.

## Middleware Contracts

| Layer | Contract |
| --- | --- |
| CORS | Explicit `DOMAINS` allowlist; no wildcard production origins |
| JWT | `Authorization: Bearer <token>` injects claims |
| Optional JWT | Enriches anonymous reads without blocking |
| MFA/IP hopping | Auth-sensitive middleware runs under `/api` |
| Permission | RBAC by permission string; admin role implies all permissions |
| Rate limit | General/auth/defcon limiters are per IP |
| Armed guard | Blocks API traffic while tenant is armed except allowlisted paths |
| API cache | JSON responses are `no-store` via `utils.WriteJSON/WriteError` |

## Auth Contracts

- JWT access tokens use HS256 and `SESSION_TIMEOUT_MIN` (default 30 minutes).
- Refresh tokens are stateful and stored in `user_sessions`.
- Passwords use bcrypt cost 12 and 8-72 character validation.
- Credentials, TOTP secrets, and backup codes are auth-owned tables, not `users`.
- Role/permission changes push `user:update permissions_changed` with a fresh token.
- MFA-required state can be pushed as `mfa:required` / user update challenge events.

