## Config Registry Routes

| Method | Path | Contract |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `GET` | `/config/sections` | Registered page-section metadata |
| `GET` | `/config/section-types/{type}` | One registered section definition |
| `GET/POST/PUT/DELETE` | `/config/section-presets...` | Preferred datasource-backed reusable preset routes; writes require service-level `home.manage` |
| Alias | `/config/custom-sections...` | Compatibility alias for section presets |
| `GET` | `/config/components`, `/config/components/{type}` | Registered render components |
| `POST` | `/config/datasources/preview` | Established `home.manage` editor preview; bounded unsaved `.ts` files and transient env input execute only through the backend TypeScript queue and return data, diagnostics, compiled output, and bounded outbound-request metadata |

Saved datasource renders continue through `POST /config/datasources/{id}/execute`.
They may use stored env values and shared result caching, but omit privileged
preview-only compiled output and outbound-request metadata.
Compile, saved execute, and protected preview jobs share one bounded datasource
conveyor manager. Deterministic per-operation datasource keys coalesce identical
saved work; unsaved/transient work uses a deterministic input hash. Followers
wait outside the worker pool for the winning result, and the winning worker
rechecks Redis before launching Node so a cache fill between dispatch and start
does not produce duplicate work. Panic recovery releases every waiter.
The execution worker uses the Node permission model and a context-owned fetch
facade; the child inherits no backend env and cannot generate code strings,
write files, spawn children/workers, load addons, or receive unbounded HTTP
payloads. Outbound requests resolve and pin public addresses, reject private or
credential-bearing destinations, and strip sensitive headers across origins.

## Store Contracts

- Product/category reads allow optional JWT; management writes require JWT plus
  service-level permission checks.
- Product prices, order totals, wallet amounts, and reference incentives are cents.
- Products include `original_price`, `stock_unlimited`, `is_active`, and
  `special_actions` JSONB exposed as JSON string.
- Cart and checkout routes are authenticated. `POST /store/checkout` accepts
  explicit server-priced items and requires an opaque `Idempotency-Key`; exact
  replays return the same order/result with `replayed=true`, while changed
  payload reuse fails closed.
- Checkout supports `delivery_cash`, `wallet`, `card_{id}`, demo provider, and
  Stripe provider depending on configuration.
- Checkout identity persists only key/payload hashes. An operation binds to one
  order before charging, reserves stock once, passes the same logical identity
  to the provider, and remains retryable after an unknown provider outcome.
- Provider lifecycle changes enter through signed, bounded Stripe callbacks or
  an owner/manager-authorized status refresh. Provider event IDs and payloads
  are hash-bound and replay-safe; payment rows are unique per order and provider
  reference.
- Wallet routes expose balance, topups, transactions, and user-managed cards.
  Internal financial effects can carry a user-and-scope operation identity;
  only its key hash and payload hash persist. Exact replays return the original
  immutable ledger row, while key reuse with a different payload fails closed.
- Reference codes pay the code owner once per order through a transaction-coupled
  wallet credit; users cannot use their own active code.
- Special actions are generic account fulfilments. Paid special-action orders
  use commit-coupled durable records before applying role and wallet effects.
  Workers use bounded attempts and expiring ownership leases; effects are
  idempotent per order item/action, malformed effects fail closed, and exhausted
  jobs require a `store.manageOrders` service-authorized retry through
  `POST /store/orders/{id}/fulfilments/retry` before completion can resume.
- Store catalog mutations broadcast `store:update`; cart and checkout outcomes
  push directly to the buyer.
- Store order mutations push `order:update` to buyers, store managers, and
  product owners; product owners also receive `store_order` notifications and
  system inbox messages with seller-scoped order details.
- Store order mutations also propagate `order:update` to the `order:{id}`
  resource subscription so open order detail/cards update across tabs in real
  time.
- DEFCON reset clears jail/history/counter pressure but must preserve trusted
  citizens; verified priority access should grant a short user bypass and promote
  the current IP to trusted.
- DEFCON telemetry classifies the adaptive allowance as `low`, `guarded`,
  `elevated`, `high`, or `critical`; clients use this server-owned threat level
  for status colors rather than duplicating limiter thresholds.
- Order item fulfillment is vendor-scoped: product owners may update only their
  own line items' `vendor_status`/`vendor_note`, stock reservation/release follows
  those lines only, and the parent order status is an aggregate such as
  `pending`, `vendor_review`, `accepted`, `rejected`, or `completed`.
- Order and vendor transitions are row-locked and state-guarded. Terminal states
  cannot regress; accepted stock is restored at most once on a negative outcome;
  and an order with a succeeded payment cannot enter a refund-like state through
  the ordinary status API.
- The product-owner selling role is named `Vendor`; legacy `store-seller` data
  should be migrated into it while preserving `store.product-seller` and
  `store.product-new` permissions.

## User Read Contracts

- `POST /api/users/batch` is a guest-safe bounded read for high-fanout rich-user
  UI. It accepts at most 50 unique positive IDs and returns explicit public
  profiles only. Public batch and individual profile DTOs exclude email,
  suspension/security state, permissions, and auth metadata.
- The authenticated `/users/profile` route remains authoritative for the
  caller's complete account/profile state.
- External identity providers are tenant-local, provider-neutral definitions.
  Link challenges are ten-minute, single-use, user/provider/access-session
  bound, and store only SHA-256 token/session bindings. Active provider subjects
  are unique per provider and never appear in public profile projections.
- `GET /api/external-identities/public/users/{userID}` returns only links the
  user made public and the provider permits publicly. It exposes provider key,
  provider label, display name, and verification time, not opaque subjects,
  internal link IDs, challenge state, adapter keys, or credentials.
- Link/reverify/unlink and visibility writes enforce established-account trust
  in the service. Provider registration requires service-level `home.manage`;
  unsupported or disabled adapters fail closed.

## Persistence Contracts

- PostgreSQL 16, per-tenant databases on shared instance.
- Pool defaults: open 100, idle 50, lifetime 30 minutes.
- Migration policy is in `.specs/migrations_spec`.
- Key tables include users/auth/session/RBAC, forum, products/cart/orders/payments,
  wallet/cards/reference payouts, inbox, notifications, site_config, pages,
  page comments, data sources, uploads/media history, analytics.
- Custom pages persist only in `pages`; datasource-backed reusable presets remain
  in `custom_sections`. Migration 035 retires the unused normalized experiment
  and blocks if response rows or palette tokens imply unexpected authority.

## Lifecycle and Trash Contracts

- Application records use lifecycle state instead of runtime hard deletion.
  Restorable entities use `deleted_at`/`deleted_by`; reversible relationships
  use `inactive_at`/`inactive_by`; sessions revoke; auth secrets are scrubbed.
- Normal reads, joins, counts, searches, and mutations require active rows and
  active ancestors. Container deletion does not tombstone descendants.
- Delete and restore write `resource_lifecycle_events`. Runtime `DELETE FROM`
  is forbidden for application records, immutable evidence, and lifecycle
  history by database triggers; only logins explicitly granted SET access to
  the NOLOGIN `skaia_hard_delete_operator` role may run reviewed cleanup.
- `/api/trash/` aggregates bounded domain-owned projections. Providers own
  authorization and SQL; the aggregator never accepts a caller-selected scope.
  Owners/deleters see their authorized tombstones, while the provider's
  manage-others permission expands only that resource domain. Hidden tombstones
  and unauthorized restores resolve as not found.
- `POST /api/trash/{resource}/{id}/restore` is transactional, audited, and
  returns `409 restore_conflict` when an active parent or unique-value conflict
  prevents restoration.
- Page palette writes stage active ordering during replacement; active
  display-order uniqueness excludes tombstoned tokens.

## Upload/SSR Contracts

- Uploads are user-isolated under `uploads/users/{userID}/{type}/`.
- Images: JPEG/PNG/WebP/GIF <= 10 MB with dimension validation.
- Videos: MP4/WebM/OGG/MOV <= 50 MB.
- Traversal (`..`) is rejected.
- `internal/seo` caches origin-independent route metadata, renders SPA head tags
  against the validated tenant `PUBLIC_BASE_URL`, returns noindex/404 for
  missing resources, and performs no outbound image fetch.
- Branding/SEO and route-resource mutations invalidate the versioned tenant SEO
  namespace. Legacy rendered `ssr:meta:` keys are removed by exact tenant prefix
  during handler startup. Live stream metadata is deliberately not cached.

## Rewards, Rankings, and Community Workflows

- `/rewards` is non-financial. Signed provider events resolve verified external
  subjects, persist hash-only replay evidence, and atomically append one grant
  and ledger credit. Redemptions use hashed idempotency, serialized immutable
  debits, and reclaimable adapter leases with operator recovery.
- `/rankings` owns typed datasets, seasons, subjects, metric direction, tie
  rules, visibility, and producer idempotency. Closed seasons reject writes;
  standings use indexed score/id cursors and omit external subject identifiers.
- `/community` shares only publication discovery fields. Proposal decisions and
  votes, showcase media/credits, and event schedules/capacity/attendance remain
  explicit records. Services own visibility, transitions, capacity, deletion,
  and Trash restoration.
- Every community publication references one unique author-owned custom page.
  Creation is transactional, `pages.content` is the sole authored body, detail
  reads pass through the page service's caller-specific interactive sanitizer,
  and page/publication deletion and restoration move together.
- Every community publication also references one unique forum thread whose
  `user_id` is the publication author. The repository creates the page, thread,
  publication, workflow evidence, and kind record in one transaction; callers
  cannot substitute an unrelated thread, and publication lifecycle carries both
  owned resources.
- Authors may edit/delete their own publications. The granular
  `community.publication-edit` and `community.publication-delete` permissions
  independently extend those actions to other users' private/draft records;
  `community.manage` owns proposal transitions and managed event creation.
  Community Trash manage-others/restore authority follows
  `community.publication-delete`. `home.manage` affects the linked page only and
  grants no community mutation.
- Proposal voting is replaceable while a published proposal is `submitted` or
  `under_review`; both service policy and the SQL mutation close voting after an
  accepted, rejected, or completed transition.
- Event attendance requires a visibility-authorized published event before work
  and repeats public/member-or-owner visibility inside the capacity transaction.
- Community mutation handlers reject malformed IDs and bounded JSON explicitly;
  missing visible publications return 404 rather than a generic internal error.

## Invariants

- Security-sensitive operations follow `.specs/security_architecture_spec`; route
  middleware and frontend checks never replace service-level policy enforcement.
- API JSON must go through `utils.WriteJSON` / `utils.WriteError`.
- Bible reads remain `no-store` like every other API JSON response; corpus
  immutability does not create a server/CDN caching exception.
- Business logic and SQL must not live in handlers.
- Auth secrets must never be cached in public user models.
- Landing page is `site_config.landing_page_slug`; there is no `is_index`.
- WebSocket pushes are part of mutation correctness, not optional decoration.
- `/health` is dependency-free liveness. `/ready` is bounded required-dependency
  readiness; operator diagnostics and incident writes use the status service.

